And it was made popular in the early 80s by Jack Welsh, who was at that point CEO of GE. And at GE, what they did was they created three different tiers, employees in the top 20%, those in the middle 70%, and those in the bottom 10%, who are more likely to be let go. In an article published in 2013, Jack Welsh said, "'Yes, I realize that some believe the bell curve aspect of differentiation is cruel. That always strikes me as odd.

16
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:36.000
We grade children in school, often as young as 9 or 10, and no one calls that cruel. But somehow adults can't take it? Explain that one to me. Stack ranking has spread to many other firms since Jack Welch and GE practiced it 40 years ago. Mary says that includes the co-working company, WeWork. The company WeWork adopted some of this stack ranking and they would let go up to 20% of their workforce annually.

